// DAS 140 and DAS 142 — the two California apprenticeship notices, kept as
// TWO TABLES because they are two documents.
//
// Part of the multi-file Prisma schema in this folder.
//
// WHAT THEY ARE, because the names give nothing away and the difference is
// the whole design:
//
//   DAS 140 — "Public Works Contract Award Information". A contractor that
//   has been awarded a public works contract NOTIFIES the apprenticeship
//   committee for each apprenticeable craft it will employ. One per award,
//   per craft, per committee. It is not a request for anybody.
//
//   DAS 142 — "Request for Dispatch of an Apprentice". A written REQUEST to
//   a committee to send an apprentice, for a stated craft, on a stated
//   date. It happens as often as the crew changes, and it has an answer: the
//   committee dispatches somebody, or it does not.
//
// WHY NOT ONE TABLE WITH A `type` FLAG. It was considered and rejected on
// four separate grounds, each of which would have had to be papered over
// with a nullable column and a branch:
//
//   1. IDENTITY. A 140 is unique on (job, committee, craft) — sending a
//      second one for the same craft is a correction, not a second notice.
//      A 142 is unique on (job, committee, craft, DATE NEEDED), because
//      asking again next month is an entirely separate request. One table
//      cannot hold both unique keys.
//   2. DEADLINE. The 140's clock starts at contract execution and runs
//      FORWARD (ten days). The 142's runs BACKWARD from the date an
//      apprentice is needed (72 hours, excluding weekends), which is not
//      even the same kind of arithmetic. See lib/das-forms.ts.
//   3. FIELDS. The 140 carries the three compliance-election boxes and the
//      contractor's estimate of journeyman and apprentice hours. The 142
//      carries a COUNT of apprentices and the date they are wanted. Neither
//      set means anything on the other form.
//   4. OUTCOME. A 142 has one — dispatched, unable to dispatch, no reply —
//      and that outcome is the contractor's defence when no apprentice ever
//      turned up. A 140 has no outcome at all.
//
// A flag would make every one of those a runtime branch on a column, and
// CLAUDE.md's rule about blurring two documents' rules into one is exactly
// what that produces.
//
// WHAT NONE OF THIS INVENTS, and it is the point of the whole area:
//
//   There is NO committee registry in this app and none is planned. DIR
//   publishes one; scraping or hand-copying it here would put a street
//   address on a document the state receives, sourced from a table nobody
//   maintains. So a committee is a row the CONTRACTOR fills in once, from
//   DIR's own lookup, and reuses — `ApprenticeshipCommittee` below. Every
//   contact field on it is nullable, and the printed form shows a blank with
//   a sentence rather than a guess. Same call PrevailingWageDetermination
//   made about wage rates and LicenseClassificationReference made about
//   Nevada licensing.
//
//   NO DEADLINE IS TREATED AS EXACT. The 72-hour rule excludes holidays and
//   is enforced to the hour; this app holds neither a California holiday
//   calendar nor a time of day (dates are UTC midnight). lib/das-forms.ts
//   therefore computes a LATEST SEND DATE that ignores holidays, and says
//   so on every screen that shows it. A deadline this app guessed, printed
//   and was wrong about is worse than no deadline.
//
// EVIDENCE RECORDS, both of them. Once `sentOn` / `requestedOn` is set the
// row is correspondence that reached a state-recognized committee: the
// action layer refuses to delete it and refuses to edit what was sent. See
// lib/actions/dasForms.ts, where that rule lives, and CLAUDE.md on evidence
// records. Every date here is ENTERED — none is stamped from a click.
//
// NEITHER MODEL HAS A SEQUENCE NUMBER, deliberately. WH-347 has one because
// the federal form has a "Payroll No." box; DAS 140 and DAS 142 have no such
// box, and minting "DAS-140-0007" would print a number on a state form that
// the state never asked for. See lib/das-forms.ts's header.
//
// The two models ARE in `HANDLED_MODELS` and in both cleanup scripts'
// `del()` order, because a required `jobId` is a RESTRICT child of `Job` and
// a scratch job carrying one could otherwise never be deleted (#227's
// shape). They are NOT in `NEVER_DELETE`: the never-delete rule is enforced
// where a person can reach it, in the action, and an operator running
// `clean-scratch-data.mjs` by hand is a different contract from a button in
// the app.

/// Which of the DAS 140's three boxes the contractor ticked.
///
/// The names are the app's, the meanings are the form's, and the mapping is
/// UNVERIFIED against a primary DIR page — see lib/das-forms.ts's citation
/// table and FEATURE-AUDIT.md. Nothing here is defaulted: an election the
/// app picked would be the app making a declaration to the state.
enum DasComplianceElection {
  /// Box 1 — already approved to train by this committee (signatory).
  APPROVED_TO_TRAIN
  /// Box 2 — will comply with this committee's standards for this project.
  WILL_COMPLY_WITH_STANDARDS
  /// Box 3 — will be governed by the California Apprenticeship Council's
  /// regulations instead.
  CAC_REGULATIONS
}

/// How the notice actually left the building. 8 CCR 230.1 names first class
/// mail, fax and email for a dispatch request; HAND_DELIVERED is here
/// because contractors do it and a record that cannot describe what
/// happened is a record that gets left blank.
enum DasTransmissionMethod {
  FIRST_CLASS_MAIL
  FAX
  EMAIL
  HAND_DELIVERED
}

/// What the committee did about a DAS 142. Null on the row means NO
/// RESPONSE HAS BEEN RECORDED, which is a different claim from
/// NO_RESPONSE — that one means somebody checked and nothing came back.
enum Das142DispatchOutcome {
  DISPATCHED
  UNABLE_TO_DISPATCH
  NO_RESPONSE
}

/// An apprenticeship committee, as this company has recorded it.
///
/// NOT a registry. DIR publishes the authoritative list of committees by
/// craft and area; this table is the handful the company actually works
/// with, typed in once from that lookup and reused on every notice. Every
/// contact field is nullable and blank prints as a blank.
///
/// Company-scoped rather than job-scoped for the obvious reason and one
/// less obvious one: the same committee receives a DAS 140 for every award
/// in its area, so a per-job copy would be the same address retyped per job
/// and free to differ between them.
model ApprenticeshipCommittee {
  id        String  @id @default(cuid())
  companyId String
  company   Company @relation(fields: [companyId], references: [id])

  /// What the committee calls itself, as written on DIR's lookup.
  name String

  /// The craft or trade this committee trains for, in the COMMITTEE's words
  /// ("Drywall/Lathers"), which is routinely not what a CBA classification
  /// is called. Free text for the same reason
  /// CompanyLicense.classificationCode is: a fixed list would be wrong for
  /// the next one.
  craftName String

  /// Optional link to one of this company's own classifications, so the job
  /// side can suggest committees for a craft that is on the job. Nullable
  /// and advisory — the craft PRINTED on a notice is the snapshot on the
  /// notice itself, never read back through here.
  craftClassificationId String?
  craftClassification   CraftClassification? @relation(fields: [craftClassificationId], references: [id])

  /// The committee's geographic area of operation, as DIR states it. 8 CCR
  /// 230 keys the obligation on whether that area includes the site of the
  /// public work, so this is the field somebody reads when deciding which
  /// committees a job's notices go to. Free text: it is a sentence about
  /// counties, not a polygon, and a geocoder would be guessing.
  geographicArea String

  /// The state program number, when the company has it. NULL MEANS NOT
  /// RECORDED and prints as a blank the contractor fills. Whether either
  /// form actually requires it is UNVERIFIED against a primary DIR page.
  programSponsorNumber String?

  /// Where the notice is sent. All nullable, none derived, none defaulted.
  /// A form with a wrong address on it is worse than one with none: the
  /// deadline is met by DELIVERY, and a plausible address that is not the
  /// committee's silently fails the requirement. There is a documented
  /// penalty for sending a DAS 142 to the wrong committee.
  addressLine1 String?
  addressLine2 String?
  city         String?
  state        String?
  postalCode   String?
  email        String?
  fax          String?
  phone        String?

  /// Whether THIS committee has approved the company to train apprentices.
  ///
  /// Load-bearing rather than descriptive, and null is a real third value.
  /// 8 CCR 230(a) sends a contractor already approved to train to the
  /// APPROVING committee only, and one not approved to ALL applicable
  /// committees in the area. So this decides how many DAS 140s an award
  /// owes — and a null must therefore read as "nobody has recorded it" and
  /// make lib/das-forms.ts decline to answer, rather than defaulting to
  /// either branch and producing a confident wrong count.
  approvedToTrainUs Boolean?

  /// Where the company read all of this. The honesty of the table: a
  /// committee address with no citation is somebody's memory of a phone
  /// call.
  sourceUrl String?
  note      String?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  das140Notices Das140Notice[]
  das142Requests Das142Request[]

  @@index([companyId])
  @@index([craftClassificationId])
}

/// One DAS 140 — this award, this craft, this committee.
///
/// Unique on (job, committee, craft) because a second notice for the same
/// three things is a correction to the first, not a second notification.
model Das140Notice {
  id    String @id @default(cuid())
  jobId String
  job   Job    @relation(fields: [jobId], references: [id])

  committeeId String
  committee   ApprenticeshipCommittee @relation(fields: [committeeId], references: [id])

  /// The craft as it was NOTIFIED, snapshotted from the committee at
  /// creation and never read back through the relation. Editing the
  /// committee row later must not rewrite what a committee was already
  /// told — that is the evidence-record rule, and a join would break it
  /// silently.
  craftName String

  /// Which of the three boxes. Required: the form has no fourth state and
  /// the app has no business choosing.
  election DasComplianceElection

  /// The date of execution of the prime contract or subcontract. ENTERED
  /// from the document, UTC midnight, never stamped — and the start of the
  /// ten-day clock, which is why it is required rather than nullable.
  contractExecutedOn DateTime

  /// The contractor's own estimates, as the form asks for them. NULL MEANS
  /// NOT ENTERED and prints as a blank.
  ///
  /// Deliberately NOT derived from the estimate's labor lines, even though
  /// this app holds them. The form asks what the contractor expects to
  /// employ on this craft; an estimate is priced work, split by cost code
  /// and not by apprentice tier, and turning one into the other would be
  /// the app making a projection in the contractor's name on a document
  /// the state keeps.
  estimatedJourneymanHours Decimal? @db.Decimal(9, 2)
  estimatedApprenticeHours Decimal? @db.Decimal(9, 2)

  /// Expected dates for this craft's work. Nullable; nothing defaults them
  /// from Job.startDate, because the job's dates are the whole job and this
  /// is one trade on it.
  estimatedStartOn      DateTime?
  estimatedCompletionOn DateTime?

  /// The subcontract amount, when the contractor chooses to state it.
  contractAmount Decimal? @db.Decimal(12, 2)

  /// The awarding body's own project number, as printed on the call for
  /// bids. Free text; nothing derives it. The job carries `awardingBody`
  /// already and that is read directly rather than copied here.
  projectIdentifier String?

  /// When the notice was actually sent. NULL = NOT SENT. Entered, not
  /// stamped: a notice posted on Friday and recorded on Monday was sent on
  /// Friday, and the ten-day test is about the first date.
  sentOn     DateTime?
  sentMethod DasTransmissionMethod?

  /// Where the proof of transmission lives — "fax confirmation in the job
  /// folder", a certified-mail number. Free text on purpose: the proof is a
  /// piece of paper somewhere, and pretending to store it would be worse
  /// than pointing at it.
  proofNote String?

  note String?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  @@unique([jobId, committeeId, craftName])
  @@index([jobId])
  @@index([committeeId])
}

/// One DAS 142 — a written request for an apprentice, for a craft, for a
/// date.
///
/// Unique on (job, committee, craft, date needed): asking the same
/// committee for the same craft on a different date is a separate request
/// with its own 72-hour test and its own answer.
model Das142Request {
  id    String @id @default(cuid())
  jobId String
  job   Job    @relation(fields: [jobId], references: [id])

  committeeId String
  committee   ApprenticeshipCommittee @relation(fields: [committeeId], references: [id])

  /// Snapshotted at creation, same reason as on Das140Notice.
  craftName String

  /// How many apprentices are wanted. Required and entered: the count is a
  /// staffing decision, and deriving one from a ratio shortfall would have
  /// the app ask a committee for people the contractor did not ask for.
  apprenticesRequested Int

  /// The date the apprentice is needed ON SITE. ENTERED, UTC midnight, and
  /// the anchor the 72-hour lead time counts BACK from.
  neededFrom DateTime
  /// Through this date, when the request covers a run of days. Nullable.
  neededTo DateTime?

  /// When the WRITTEN request was actually sent. NULL = NOT SENT YET, which
  /// is the state a request sits in while somebody is still deciding. The
  /// lead-time test compares this against `neededFrom` and nothing else.
  requestedOn DateTime?
  sentMethod  DasTransmissionMethod?

  projectIdentifier String?
  proofNote         String?

  /// What came back. `respondedOn` null and `outcome` null is "nothing
  /// recorded"; `outcome = NO_RESPONSE` is "somebody checked and nothing
  /// came back", which is the version that defends a contractor who ran
  /// short. Two different facts, two different columns.
  respondedOn DateTime?
  outcome     Das142DispatchOutcome?
  outcomeNote String?

  note String?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  @@unique([jobId, committeeId, craftName, neededFrom])
  @@index([jobId])
  @@index([committeeId])
}
